# Flow References

Flow expressions map data into node parameters and the Flow output. A
reference is stored data. It is not an Elixir function call at runtime.

Run the code cells from top to bottom.

## Install Jido Action

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido_action, "~> 3.0.0-rc.1"}])
```

## Define An Echo Action

```elixir
defmodule FlowReferences.Echo do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "flow_references_echo",
    schema: Zoi.map(),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  @impl true
  def run(params, _context), do: {:ok, params}
end
```

## Read Flow Input With `input`

`input(path)` reads the map passed as Flow input. A path can be an atom,
string, integer, or a list of these segments.

```elixir
defmodule FlowReferences.InputExample do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "flow_references_input",
    schema: Zoi.map(),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  flow do
    step "echo",
      action: FlowReferences.Echo,
      params: %{
        account: input(:account),
        nested_id: input([:account, :id]),
        string_id: input(["account", "id"])
      }
  end
end

{:ok, result} =
  Jido.Exec.run(
    FlowReferences.InputExample,
    %{"account" => %{"id" => "acct-1"}},
    %{}
  )

%{account: %{"id" => "acct-1"}, nested_id: "acct-1", string_id: "acct-1"} = result
```

Map lookup can find a string key when the path segment is an atom. List paths
use non-negative integer segments.

## Read Runtime Context With `context`

`context(path)` reads the separate runtime context map. Use it for caller-owned
data such as a tenant or trace identifier.

```elixir
defmodule FlowReferences.ContextExample do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "flow_references_context",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{value: Zoi.integer()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  flow do
    step "echo",
      action: FlowReferences.Echo,
      params: %{
        value: input(:value),
        tenant_id: context([:tenant, :id]),
        trace_id: context(:trace_id)
      }
  end
end

{:ok, %{value: 3, tenant_id: "tenant-1", trace_id: "trace-1"}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(
    FlowReferences.ContextExample,
    %{value: 3},
    %{tenant: %{id: "tenant-1"}, trace_id: "trace-1"}
  )
```

Context references do not create graph dependencies.

## Write Literals Directly

Normal Elixir literals stay literal Flow data. Maps and lists can contain both
literals and references.

```elixir
defmodule FlowReferences.LiteralExample do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "flow_references_literal",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{value: Zoi.integer()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  flow do
    step "echo",
      action: FlowReferences.Echo,
      params: %{
        value: input(:value),
        label: :constant,
        options: %{mode: :safe},
        flags: [true, false]
      }
  end
end

{:ok, %{value: 3, label: :constant, options: %{mode: :safe}}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(FlowReferences.LiteralExample, %{value: 3}, %{})
```

`value(literal)` wraps supported scalar, list, and map literals. Tuples and
executable expressions are not part of the Flow module DSL. Normal literals do
not need `value/1`.

This is an intentional portability rule. Use maps instead of keyword lists,
and use lists instead of tuples. Put computation in an Action. Low-level
canonical Flow values can contain some static BEAM terms, but stored Map or
JSON data rejects values outside the portable scalar, map, and list subset.

## Read Prior Results With `result`

`result(node)` reads a complete node result. `result(node, path)` reads a path
directly. Each result reference creates a dependency.

```elixir
defmodule FlowReferences.ResultExample do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "flow_references_result",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{value: Zoi.integer()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  flow do
    step "source",
      action: FlowReferences.Echo,
      params: %{value: input(:value)}

    step "copy",
      action: FlowReferences.Echo,
      params: %{
        direct: result("source", :value),
        complete: result("source")
      }
  end
end

{:ok, %{direct: 9, complete: %{value: 9}}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(FlowReferences.ResultExample, %{value: 9}, %{})
```

## Project Values With `select`

`select(source, path)` projects a path from another expression. It is useful
when the source is already composed or when a list index is part of the path.

```elixir
defmodule FlowReferences.SelectExample do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "flow_references_select",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{value: Zoi.integer()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  flow do
    step "source",
      action: FlowReferences.Echo,
      params: %{
        payload: %{nested: %{value: input(:value)}},
        items: [:first, :second, input(:value)]
      }

    output %{
      nested_value: select(result("source"), [:payload, :nested, :value]),
      list_item: select(result("source"), [:items, 1])
    }
  end
end

{:ok, %{nested_value: 6, list_item: :second}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(FlowReferences.SelectExample, %{value: 6}, %{})
```

## Missing Paths Are Errors

When a map key or list element is missing, execution returns a non-retryable
error. The error identifies the reference type, full path, failed segment, and
resolved path prefix. This rule prevents a spelling error from silently
becoming `nil`. A key or list element that exists and contains `nil` still
resolves to `nil`.

```elixir
defmodule FlowReferences.MissingPath do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "flow_references_missing",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{value: Zoi.integer()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  flow do
    step "source",
      action: FlowReferences.Echo,
      params: %{value: input(:value)}

    output %{
      missing_input: select(input(:missing), :nested),
      missing_result: select(result("source"), [:missing, :nested])
    }
  end
end

{:error, error} = Jido.Exec.run(FlowReferences.MissingPath, %{value: 1}, %{})

"flow reference path does not exist" = Exception.message(error)
:input = error.details.ref_type
[:missing, :nested] = error.details.path
[] = error.details.resolved_path
false = Jido.Action.Error.retryable?(error)
```

## Use Scoped References

Some references exist only inside one node type:

| Reference | Valid DSL field |
| --- | --- |
| `item(path)` | Map and Reduce `params`. |
| `item_index()` | Map and Reduce `params`. |
| `item_id()` | Map and Reduce `params`. |
| `accumulator(path)` | Reduce `params`. |
| `state(path)` | Iterate `params`, `update`, and `while`. |
| `iteration_index()` | Iterate `params`, `update`, and `while`. |
| `body_result(path)` | Iterate `params`, `update`, and `while`. |

The compiler rejects a scoped reference when its runtime value does not exist.
For example, `item()` is not valid in a Map `collection`, and `state()` is not
valid in Iterate initial State.

See [Map and Reduce](flow-collections.livemd) and [Iterate and
State](flow-iterate-state.livemd) for complete examples.
